 could be done much much faster we just  shouldn't have these delays we should  have something proper in place that can  let families reunite at such an awful  time i mean the comparison to hong kong  quite fair i mean would you want there  to be sent to set up at every single  entry point into the uk because that  would be unworkable wouldn't it  it's i mean obviously every country is  different so the issues the minister  himself said yesterday that they were  looking at whether they could use the  technology that they used for hong kong  well look faster i mean why wasn't this  being done  weeks ago when british intelligence were  warning for some time that an invasion  from russia into ukraine was imminent  and that's the thing they didn't put  that planning in place they haven't set  up enough emergency centers and so  you've got this awful situation where  families make it all the way to calais  you know someone who's driven their  niece who they went to pick up had  driven all the way back to cali and then  being sent to brussels where the center  is only open three days a week i mean  how can that be  when there is a war on in europe that  you have you're one of your  centers that is close to closest to  calais only open three days a week and  that's the thing the home office really  should be getting a grip on and it is  shambolic and shameful that they have  failed to do something so basic now in  regards to that the home office will be  saying that it's the security element  that you need to be careful of it's  being uh talked about this morning the  kremlin operatives masquerading as  ukrainians i mean your shadow home  secretary you wouldn't want those  criminals coming in at all so there has  to be some checks in place don't you and  i'm saying do the security checks but  you can do these recent security checks  on the spot they're really quick to do  well if you talk to border force  officials you talk to people who do  funding  this is basic stuff to do that they  should be able to do if you look at what  other countries are doing to support  ukraine you know obviously poland  hungary they're doing the most because  they are the neighboring countries and  that is where people are wanting to stay  but really it should not be beyond the  width of the home office to make sure  they've got enough staff in place to do  the security checks they've got the  technology to do it they should be able  to do this on the spot and do this much  more quickly the problem is what we've  seen from the home office is they just  resisted this all the way along they're  still saying some family members for  example if you are  a health care worker if you work in a  british hospital you're ukrainian here  on a healthcare visa you can't bring  your elderly mom to come and live with  you even if she's fled keith or she's  fled bombardment in ukraine and that's  not right i don't think that's what  people i mean we've seen of course we've  seen this war coming what do you put  this down to then the fact that it does  seem to be so difficult for the home  office to get a grip of the situation  because people are struggling on the  border to be honest i don't get it  because other departments have been  planning other departments have been  working on this and preparing and you  know  we've worked because with the the  foreign office with other departments we  have been pressing the treasury and  other departments to go much further and  faster with sanctions but we are working  with them to do this it's the home  office that seems to have been so  resistant it tried to use the original  visa system if you remember we had that  awful moment when the home office  minister said well people should come  here and apply for a seasonal  agricultural visa they should come and  pick fruit well that's clearly not the  answer when you've got desperate  families fleeing conflict is this the  result of having that tough border  policy that has been 